{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["מסתלל — hitpa'el from (Isaiah 62) סולו סולו המסילה. 'כובש אותם לעבדים' — subdues them as slaves, as written: (Jeremiah 34) ויכבשם לעבדים ולשפחות. And to teach you the pattern of the hitpa'el: all hitpa'el forms take an added tav at the beginning, except those that I will specify below as exceptions. From אמר: יאמר — (Psalms 94) יתאמרו כל פועלי און. יתברך. יתגדל. (Ezekiel 38) והתגדלתי והתקדשתי. יתהלל. המתהלל. יתוכח. ויתחבא. לשוא תתיפי. (Isaiah 28:20) והמסכה צרה. כהתכנס. יתלכדו. ויתמודד. מתנשא. ויתלקטו. התעללתי. מתפלל. מתרושש. והון רב. — Here you have the entire alef-bet with tav at their head, except for these seven letters: dalet, zayin, tet, samekh, tsadi, shin, tav. For shin and samekh, the tav comes after them in their hitpa'el: from סל — מסתולל. From שר — תשתרר עלינו. From שבח — ישתבח. From שמר — (Micah 6:16) וישתמר חקות עמרי. From שכר — משתכר. For tsadi and zayin, the tav of hitpa'el does not come at the beginning but after the tsadi a tet comes in place of the tav of hitpa'el: from צדק — one says נצטדק. From צבע — יצטבע in Daniel. From ציד — הצטיידנו in Joshua. From צרף — מצטרף. And after zayin a dalet comes in place of the tav of hitpa'el: from זמן — הזדמנתון in Daniel. But these are additions within the words: the tet of נצטדק, the dalet of הזדמנתון. And the tav of יתאמרו serves to make it hitpa'el as I explained, and so too all of them. But the letters themselves, when they stand at the head of the word, have no added letter for the hitpa'el — as in: וישמע את הקול מדבר אליו — one would not say (variant: like) מתדבר. לא יטמא בעל — (one would not say) like יתטמא. ועם עקש (ופתלתל) (ms.: תתפתל) (variant: תתפל) — one would not say (תתתפתל). תתמם — one would not say תתתמם."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "Rashbam systematically catalogues which guttural and sibilant letters cause the tav of the hitpa'el binyan to assimilate or change position/form. Several embedded biblical citations are unaddressed in the original. The variants marked with נ\"א and צ\"ל are rendered as 'variant' and 'ms.' respectively. The comment addresses the morphological analysis of מסתלל in Exodus 9:17 but opens from the lemma onward into the grammatical treatise."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:41:01.951816220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}